<div class="paper-entry mb-12">
    <h3 class="text-xl mb-3">
        Filling the Gaps?: Private Sector Response to the Push and Pull
        of the Indian Public Schooling System
    </h3>
    
    <p class="font-medium text-gray-400 mb-4">Prerna Dewan, Subhasish Dey, Kasturi Kandalam, Abhiroop Mukhopadhyay, Soham Sahoo</p>

    <details class="group mb-4">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer text-sm text-accent hover:underline flex items-center gap-1 w-fit">
            <span class="transform transition-transform group-open:rotate-90">▶</span>
            Abstract
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed leading-relaxed text-sm text-gray-300 border-l-2 border-gray-600 pl-4">
            We study how improvements in public school quality shape the evolution of local schooling markets
            in India. Combining district–year ASER test scores with the universe of UDISE administrative data
            for 2012–2017, we document a robust and economically meaningful inverted-U relationship between
            public school quality and private school presence. When public schools perform poorly, marginal
            improvements in public test scores are associated with more private school entry, a larger stock of
            private schools, and a rising private–public enrollment ratio. Beyond a threshold, the relationship
            reverses: additional gains in public school quality coincide with fewer new private schools, slower
            growth in the stock of private schools, and declining private enrollment shares. We investigate
            mechanisms by examining the quality margins along which private schools adjust. As public school
            quality improves, private schools that remain in the market invest in more qualified teachers and
            better infrastructure, both of which are costly. These convex responses are consistent with a simple
            mechanism; when public schools are weak, private providers can operate at low cost and high
            enrollment; as public schools improve, private schools face declining demand and must upgrade
            quality to remain viable. Taken together, our results illustrate how public-sector performance
            shapes the supply of private schooling in a mixed education system.
        </p>
    </details>

    <p class="mb-4 text-sm">
        <strong>Keywords</strong>: Private schools, Public schools, Evolution of market for education
    </p>

    <div class="flex gap-4">
        <a href="/kastkan/papers/The_Push_and_Pull_of_Public_Education_Quality.pdf" 
           class="inline-flex items-center px-4 py-2 rounded transition-colors hover:opacity-80"
           style="background-color: rgba(100, 143, 216, 0.1);"
           target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            📄 Draft
        </a>
    </div>
</div>


<div class="paper-entry mb-12">
    <h3 class="text-xl mb-3">
        Where have all the men gone? Temporary Migration & Rural Public Works - A Discontinuity Approach
    </h3>
    
    <p class="font-medium text-gray-400 mb-4">Kasturi Kandalam</p>

    <details class="group mb-4">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer text-sm text-accent hover:underline flex items-center gap-1 w-fit">
            <span class="transform transition-transform group-open:rotate-90">▶</span>
            Abstract
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed leading-relaxed text-sm text-gray-300 border-l-2 border-gray-600 pl-4">
            This paper examines the impact of the National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (NREGS) on
            temporary migration in India, using a fuzzy regression discontinuity design that utilises the phased
            rollout of the program. Drawing on nationally representative data from the NSS Employment–Unemployment
            Survey (2007–08), the study finds that the introduction of NREGS significantly increased the seasonal
            migration of young males, while the effect on young females was negligible. The robustness of the
            findings is confirmed across different model specifications, bandwidths, and by using both sharp and
            fuzzy designs. The results suggest that NREGS enabled male migration by either substituting female
            labor into public works or by reducing the risks associated with migration through the availability
            of fallback employment. These findings highlight the complex and sometimes unintended consequences
            of rural workfare programs on household labor allocation and migration strategies.
        </p>
    </details>

    <p class="mb-4 text-sm">
        <strong>Keywords</strong>: Migration, MGNREGA, Public Works, Fuzzy RDD, India
    </p>

    <div class="flex gap-4">
        <a href="/kastkan/papers/migration_nregs_draft.pdf" 
           class="inline-flex items-center px-4 py-2 rounded transition-colors hover:opacity-80"
           style="background-color: rgba(100, 143, 216, 0.1);"
           target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            📄 Draft
        </a>
    </div>
</div>


<div class="paper-entry mb-12 pb-12 border-b border-gray-700">
    <h3 class="text-xl mb-3">
        From Charminar to Cyberabad: Political Economy of Software Industry in Erstwhile Unified Andhra Pradesh
    </h3>

    <p class="font-medium text-gray-400 mb-4">Kasturi Kandalam</p>

    <details class="group mb-4">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer text-sm text-accent hover:underline flex items-center gap-1 w-fit">
            <span class="transform transition-transform group-open:rotate-90">▶</span>
            Abstract
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed leading-relaxed text-sm text-gray-300 border-l-2 border-gray-600 pl-4">
            This paper draws on primary qualitative data and secondary quantitative data from erstwhile unified
            Andhra Pradesh to explicate how the Information and Communication Technology (ICT) policies at the
            central and state levels led to a rise in the Information Technology (IT) ecosystem. Using
            mixed-methods, I elucidate how IT industry was fostered in AP. Forty-five semi-structured interviews
            were carried out with a cross section of various stakeholders in the industry. A political economy
            framework was adopted to explicate how AP's policies changed the state's dynamic by mobilising
            labour, capital and played a significant role in making the job of "techies" aspirational.
        </p>
    </details>

    <p class="mb-4 text-sm">
        <strong>Keywords</strong>: Information Technology (IT), State reforms, IT Education, Capital mobilization, Andhra Pradesh
    </p>

    <div class="flex gap-4">
        <a href="/kastkan/papers/software_industry_ap_draft.pdf" 
